#edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y⟩$ and the points $O(0,0)$ and $A(\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $F$ is conservative.\n- **II.** ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=1,$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$\n- **III.** ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4},$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the curve $y=\\sqrt{x},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186259847 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "769cb3f6-44e5-5633-bebf-2f89e5fbe161" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259847 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $F$ is conservative, there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$ Reconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2})-f(0,0) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\cdot 1-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $F$ is conservative and ${C}_{2}$ has the same initial and terminal points as ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259851 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259181 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259851 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125735" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259851 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259851 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-2,1)$ to $(0,3)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259851 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "30e624bb-f7e4-561a-be9c-3d2d751b4f13" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259851 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-2,1)$ and $B(0,3).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(0,3)-f(-2,1) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{{0}^{2}+1}-\\frac{1}{(-2)^{2}+1} \\\\  & =3-\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259852 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259852 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125737" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259852 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259852 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y,z)={x}^{3}-{y}^{2}+z.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(1,0,3)$ to $(0,-1,2)$ in ${R}^{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259852 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "536580f0-4841-5ab4-989a-c614cdea00c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259852 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(1,0,3)$ and $B(0,-1,2).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(0,-1,2)-f(1,0,3) \\\\  & =({0}^{3}-(-1)^{2}+2)-({1}^{3}-{0}^{2}+3) \\\\  & =1-4 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259854 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259854 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125739" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259854 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259854 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y,z)=yz-x.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-1,2,0)$ to $(-2,3,1)$ in ${R}^{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259854 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a8bad440-9dfc-5258-b308-3c0a302cd41a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259854 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-1,2,0)$ and $B(-2,3,1).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(-2,3,1)-f(-1,2,0) \\\\  & =(3(1)-(-2))-(2(0)-(-1)) \\\\  & =5-1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259858 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259858 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125753" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259858 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259858 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=2x{e}^{y}\\,i+{x}^{2}{e}^{y}\\,j.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-1,0)$ to $(2,1)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259858 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e5993ea8-c19e-5b1a-a8cf-e4077a8ec7f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259858 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-1,0)$ and $B(2,1).$\n\nNotice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x{e}^{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf={x}^{2}{e}^{y}.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(2,1)-f(-1,0) \\\\  & =({2}^{2}){e}^{1}-(-1)^{2}{e}^{0} \\\\  & =4e-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259860 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259348 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259860 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125757" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259860 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259860 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=(3y-x)\\,i+3x\\,j.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(0,1)$ to $(2,-2)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259860 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8758b7de-dff9-5980-9e4d-360f6523aac9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259860 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(0,1)$ and $B(2,-2).$\n\nNotice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=3xy-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(2,-2)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =(3(2)(-2)-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2})-(3(0)(1)-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-12-2-0 \\\\  & =-14.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259864 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449160 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186259864 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449161 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186259864 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449162 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186259864 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449163 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186259864 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125793" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259864 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259864 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨{y}^{2},\\,-x⟩$ and the points $O(0,0)$ and $A(4,2)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{5}{6},$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$\n- **II.** ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{16}{3},$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the curve $y=\\sqrt{x},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$\n- **III.** ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is independent of path.\n- **IV.** $F$ is conservative." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186259864 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "da972c83-042d-5f07-b576-d951f35ad923" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259864 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Parameterizing the line segment, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{1}(t)=⟨4t,\\,2t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨4{t}^{2},\\,-4t⟩\\cdot ⟨4,\\,2⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}16{t}^{2}-8t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}2{t}^{2}-t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{[\\frac{2{t}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =8[\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Parameterizing the curve, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{2}(t)=⟨{t}^{2},\\,t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}⟨{t}^{2},\\,-{t}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨2t,\\,1⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\frac{{t}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{2}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{{2}^{3}}{3})-0 \\\\  & =8-\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ have the same initial and terminal points, yet\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\ne {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$\n\nwe conclude that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is path-dependent.\n\n- Statement IV is false. If a vector field is conservative then it is path-independent. Since $F$ is path-dependent, it is not conservative." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186408166 #edb/ref 10 :answer-field.type/checkbox #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186415197 #edb/ref 1080 "2e-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415197 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415197 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "09235254-4d47-5c19-a8cc-f2c8d86165e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415202 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415202 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415202 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1f497857-d2ea-515b-ac70-f83db9b8d663" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415205 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{5-e}{3e}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415205 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415205 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "260ed446-43fe-5beb-ab7d-78b6125c1cd3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415209 #edb/ref 1080 "3\\sqrt{2}-5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415209 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415209 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "394650d1-8e19-51ba-a71f-b0fc64e540e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415212 #edb/ref 1080 "-2e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415212 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415212 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "43de946b-6276-5f5e-981d-3f9e49ef0b6b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415215 #edb/ref 1080 "2({e}^{2}-1)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415215 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415215 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "50955a5e-a870-5833-935b-0d75056ca130" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415216 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1-2{e}^{2}}{e}" #edb/ref 17592186045417]