6 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415271 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415271 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186415197 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415271 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "eeed41f2-bb2a-5596-8fa5-7363fbf67f7c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415272 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454418 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186415272 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454419 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186415272 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454420 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186415272 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125743" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415272 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415272 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y⟩$ and the points $O(0,0)$ and $A(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $F$ is conservative.\n\n- **II.** ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=e,$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$\n\n- **III.** ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2e-1,$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the curve $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\sqrt{x},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186415272 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0027cfb5-d932-549f-8cc6-973642895e8d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415272 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $F$ is conservative, there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$ Reconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})-f(0,0) \\\\  & ={e}^{1}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-{e}^{0}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =e\\cdot 1-0 \\\\  & =e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $F$ is conservative and ${C}_{2}$ has the same initial and terminal points as ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=e.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415273 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415265 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415273 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125756" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415273 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415273 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}\\,i+\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}}\\,j.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(2,0)$ to $(3,-2)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415273 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "13b80d0a-868f-5180-a5b8-148925e3a0e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415273 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(2,0)$ and $B(3,-2).$\n\nNotice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=x\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(3,-2)-f(2,0) \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+1}-2\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{5}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415275 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454412 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186415275 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454413 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186415275 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454414 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186415275 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125720" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415275 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415275 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨y,\\,x⟩$ and the points $O(0,0)$ and $A(\\sqrt{2},2)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $F$ is conservative.\n\n- **II.** ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2\\sqrt{2},$ where ${C}_{1}$ is 